AHS-012 — Cognitive State Model

Document ID: AHS-012
Title: Cognitive State Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Process, Information

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical cognitive state model of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Cognitive State Model establishes the lifecycle and state transitions of engineering cognitive artifacts as they evolve from initial observation to validated decision support.

It enables deterministic cognition, lifecycle management, auditability, and explainability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Cognitive states
State transitions
Transition rules
State invariants
State validation
State history

This specification applies to all canonical engineering artifacts defined by ACC.

3. Canonical Definition

A Cognitive State is the current maturity and validation status of an engineering cognitive artifact during its lifecycle within Atlas.

A state describes what the artifact currently is, not what process is executing.

4. Objectives

The Cognitive State Model SHALL:

Standardize artifact maturity.
Prevent invalid state transitions.
Preserve cognitive integrity.
Support traceability.
Support explainability.
Enable lifecycle auditing.
5. Canonical Cognitive Lifecycle

Every engineering artifact SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Created
    │
Observed
    │
Validated
    │
Understood
    │
Preserved
    │
Generalized
    │
Reasoned
    │
Applied
    │
Communicated
    │
Archived

Not every artifact must reach every state, but transitions SHALL follow the defined ordering unless explicitly permitted.

6. State Definitions
Created

The artifact has been instantiated but has not yet participated in engineering cognition.

Observed

The artifact represents an engineering observation awaiting validation.

Validated

The artifact has been verified and accepted as Engineering Evidence.

Understood

Engineering meaning has been established.

Preserved

The understanding has been stored as Engineering Memory.

Generalized

Reusable Engineering Knowledge has been derived.

Reasoned

Engineering conclusions have been produced.

Applied

Engineering capabilities have acted upon the conclusions.

Communicated

Decision Support has presented the outcome.

Archived

The artifact is retained for historical reference but no longer participates in active cognition.

7. State Transition Rules

Permitted transitions:

Created
   │
Observed
   │
Validated
   │
Understood
   │
Preserved
   │
Generalized
   │
Reasoned
   │
Applied
   │
Communicated
   │
Archived

Backward transitions SHALL NOT occur except through explicit refinement workflows.

8. Refinement

Engineering cognition is iterative.

Artifacts MAY return to an earlier maturity stage through a controlled refinement process.

Example:

Reasoned
     │
New Evidence
     │
     ▼
Validated
     │
Understood
     │
Reasoned

The original artifact SHALL remain traceable.

The refinement SHALL create a new artifact version.

9. State Invariants

Every cognitive state SHALL preserve:

Artifact identity
Context
Intent
Constraints
Provenance
Traceability
Version history

These attributes SHALL remain available throughout the artifact lifecycle.

10. State Metadata

Every state SHALL record:

State identifier
Entered timestamp
Entered by
Exit timestamp
Previous state
Transition reason
Validation status
Confidence
Version
11. State Validation

A transition SHALL occur only if:

Preconditions are satisfied.
Required artifacts exist.
Mandatory metadata is present.
Constraints are satisfied.
Confidence requirements are met where applicable.

Invalid transitions SHALL be rejected.

12. State History

Atlas SHALL maintain the complete history of state transitions.

Example:

Observation #124

Created

↓

Observed

↓

Validated

↓

Understood

↓

Preserved

↓

Generalized

State history SHALL be immutable.

13. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Implements the canonical state model.
Prevents invalid transitions.
Preserves state history.
Maintains artifact identity.
Supports refinement without losing provenance.
14. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Domain-specific cognitive states.
Specialized validation states.
Multi-agent cognitive synchronization states.
Collaborative cognition states.

Such extensions SHALL remain compatible with the canonical lifecycle.